# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 6 — B, kinematics.  A velocity-time graph with three stages: the average is
#     the area over the total time, not the peak.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 6, "id": "S06-06", "module": "B", "diff": 3,
    "topic": "Average velocity from a velocity-time graph: the area under the graph divided by the whole time, expressed as a fraction of the peak speed",
    "rel": [("B", "The area under a velocity-time graph is the displacement"),
            ("B", "Average velocity is the total displacement divided by the total time, not the mean of the speeds"),
            ("B", "A triangular region of a velocity-time graph contributes half the rectangle that encloses it"),
            ("A", "Expressing the answer as a fraction of a named quantity avoids committing to a value for it")],
    "key": ["grapharea", "averagevelocity", "trapezium", "fraction"],
    "stem": '<p>The graph shows the velocity of a cyclist over a 12 s interval. The speed rises uniformly from rest to 8.0 m s<sup>&#8722;1</sup> in the first 4.0 s, stays approximately constant at 8.0 m s<sup>&#8722;1</sup> until 10 s, and then falls uniformly to rest at 12 s.</p>{{FIG:s06-06}}<p>Taking the constant speed as v, what is the cyclist&#8217;s average velocity over the 12 s?</p>',
    "opts": ['v/2', '3v/4', '2v/3', '5v/6', 'v'],
    "ans": 1,
    "distractors": ['reads the graph as a single triangle, which halves the peak',
                    'correct',
                    'takes the middle rectangle as 5.0 s long, giving 64 m over the 12 s',
                    'counts the final ramp as a rectangle as well, giving 80 m over the 12 s',
                    'counts all three stages as rectangles, which is what makes the peak look like the average'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "recognise that the displacement is the area under the graph and that the 12 s is the total time"),
            ("solve", "split the area into two triangles and one rectangle"),
            ("solve", "write each area in terms of the peak speed, using the times read from the axis"),
            ("solve", "add the areas and divide by the total time")
        
        ],
        "relations": ["the first triangle has area one half times 4.0 s times v, which is 2v",
                      "the rectangle has area 6.0 s times v, which is 6v",
                      "the second triangle has area one half times 2.0 s times v, which is v",
                      "the total area is 9v, and dividing by 12 s gives three quarters of v"],
        "insight": "Average velocity is the area over the total time. With the numbers left symbolic the arithmetic collapses to a single fraction, and the fraction is what the question is really testing: whether you have counted the two ramps as triangles rather than as rectangles.",
        "shape": "graph-reading",
        "approx": True,
        "symbolic": True,
        "figure_essential": True,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "(0.5*4*8 + 6*8 + 0.5*2*8)/12/8", "want": "3/4"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you use the area under the graph rather than the peak, and whether you can carry the peak through as a symbol so that the arithmetic stays clean.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — the rule.</b> Average velocity is the total displacement over the total time. The displacement is the area under a velocity-time graph, and the total time is the whole 12 s, including the parts where the cyclist is slow.</p>
<div class="formula">v_avg = area / total time</div>
<p><b>Step 2 — split the area.</b> The shape is a trapezium with a flat top, which splits into two triangles and a rectangle. The cyclist&#8217;s speed is approximately constant between 4.0 s and 10 s, so the middle region is a rectangle rather than a curve.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — each area in terms of v.</b></p>
<div class="formula">triangle 1: 0.5 &#215; 4.0 &#215; v = 2v
rectangle:  6.0 &#215; v = 6v
triangle 2: 0.5 &#215; 2.0 &#215; v = v</div>
<p>Each triangle contributes half of the rectangle that would enclose it. That is the discriminator of the figure: a candidate who counts each ramp as a rectangle gets 4v + 6v + 2v = 12v, and 12v/12 s = v, which is the last option.</p>
<p><b>Step 4 — add and divide.</b></p>
<div class="formula">total area = 2v + 6v + v = 9v
v_avg = 9v / 12 = 3v/4</div>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> Three quarters of the peak is below the peak, which it must be, because the cyclist is below the peak for four of the twelve seconds. And it is well above a half, because the cyclist spends half the interval at the full peak.</p>
<p>With the numbers put in, the displacement is 9 &#215; 8.0 = 72 m and the average velocity is 72/12 = 6.0 m s<sup>&#8722;1</sup>. Notice that the question did not need the 8.0: the answer is a fraction of v, and the fraction is the physics.</p>
<p><b>Answer: B</b></p>''',
    "trap": "The trap is treating the average as the peak, which happens whenever the two ramps are counted as rectangles. The second trap is dividing by the time spent at the peak rather than by the whole interval: 72 m over 10 s gives 7.2 m s&#8722;1, which is also an option in a variant of this question.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 7 — C, momentum.  Equal masses, elastic, one at rest: the outgoing paths
#     are perpendicular, and the equal-angle case forces 45 degrees.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 7, "id": "S06-07", "module": "C", "diff": 3,
    "topic": "An elastic collision between equal masses, one at rest, where the two outgoing directions are symmetric: finding the angle",
    "rel": [("C", "Momentum is conserved as a vector, so it must be conserved along the original direction and perpendicular to it"),
            ("C", "Kinetic energy is conserved in an elastic collision, which fixes the outgoing speeds"),
            ("A", "A collision with a symmetry axis can be resolved along that axis, turning a vector problem into two scalar ones"),
            ("A", "Dividing one conservation equation by another eliminates the unknown speed and leaves the angle")],
    "key": ["elastic", "momentum", "symmetry", "angle"],
    "stem": '<p>A particle of mass m travelling with speed u strikes an identical particle at rest. The collision is elastic, and the two particles emerge symmetrically, each at the same angle &#952; to the original direction and each with the same speed v.</p>{{FIG:s06-07}}<p>What is &#952;?</p>',
    "opts": ['&#952; = 45&#176;', '&#952; = 60&#176;', '&#952; = 90&#176;', '&#952; = 30&#176;', '&#952; = 22.5&#176;'],
    "ans": 0,
    "distractors": ['correct',
                    'sets the outgoing speed equal to the incoming speed, which shares the momentum equally but loses half the kinetic energy',
                    'reads the perpendicularity of the two outgoing paths as &#952; = 90&#176; instead of as the sum of the two angles being 90&#176;',
                    'mismatches the energy equation by one power of v, leaving u = &#8730;3 v and so cos &#952; = &#8730;3/2',
                    'bisects the right angle between the two outgoing paths twice, giving a quarter of 90&#176;'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "resolve the momentum conservation along the original direction and perpendicular to it"),
            ("solve", "note that the perpendicular components cancel by symmetry, which is why the two angles must be equal"),
            ("solve", "use the momentum equation along the original direction to relate u, v and the angle"),
            ("solve", "use the conservation of kinetic energy to relate u and v without the angle"),
            ("solve", "divide the two equations to eliminate v and leave a single equation for the angle"),
            ("check", "confirm the answer by putting it back: if &#952; = 45&#176; then v = u/&#8730;2, and the energy equation is satisfied"),
        ],
        "relations": ["along the original direction: mu = 2mv cos &#952;",
                      "perpendicular to it: mv sin &#952; &#8722; mv sin &#952; = 0, which is satisfied for any &#952; because the two paths are symmetric",
                      "kinetic energy: one half m u squared = 2 &#215; one half m v squared, so u = &#8730;2 v",
                      "substituting gives &#8730;2 v = 2v cos &#952;, so cos &#952; = &#8730;2/2 and &#952; = 45&#176;"],
        "insight": "Two conservation laws and two unknowns, so dividing one equation by the other removes the speed and leaves the angle. The symmetry is what makes the perpendicular equation automatic: with equal masses and one initially at rest, the perpendicular momenta must cancel, which forces the two angles to be equal in the first place.",
        "shape": "conservation",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": True,
        "figure_essential": True,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "acos(sqrt(2)/2)", "want": "pi/4"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you use both conservation laws and then eliminate the unknown speed between them. Using only one of them leaves an equation with two unknowns, which is where most candidates stop.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — resolve the momentum.</b> Momentum is a vector, so conserving it means conserving each component separately. Take the original direction as the first axis and the perpendicular as the second.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — the perpendicular equation.</b> The incoming particle has no perpendicular momentum. The two outgoing particles have equal masses and equal speeds, and they emerge on opposite sides, so their perpendicular momenta are equal and opposite:</p>
<div class="formula">mv sin &#952; &#8722; mv sin &#952; = 0</div>
<p>This is satisfied for any &#952;. It tells us nothing about the angle &#8212; but it does confirm that the symmetry assumed in the question is self-consistent, which is worth knowing before relying on it.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — the momentum along the original direction.</b> Each outgoing particle contributes v cos &#952; along the original direction:</p>
<div class="formula">mu = 2mv cos &#952;      so      u = 2v cos &#952;</div>
<p><b>Step 4 — the kinetic energy.</b> The collision is elastic, so</p>
<div class="formula">one half m u&#178; = 2 &#215; one half m v&#178;      so      u&#178; = 2v&#178;      and      u = &#8730;2 v</div>
<p><b>Step 5 — divide to eliminate v.</b> Substitute u = &#8730;2 v into the momentum equation:</p>
<div class="formula">&#8730;2 v = 2v cos &#952;      so      cos &#952; = &#8730;2/2</div>
<p>The speed v has cancelled, which is the whole point of using both laws: neither equation alone fixes the angle, but together they do, because the angle appears in one and not the other.</p>
<p><b>Step 6 — the angle.</b> The value &#8730;2/2 is the cosine of 45&#176;, and it is worth knowing by heart, because 45&#176; is the one angle for which the sine and the cosine are equal. So &#952; = 45&#176;.</p>
<p><b>A check.</b> Put the angle back into both laws. With &#952; = 45&#176; the momentum equation gives u = 2v cos 45&#176; = 2v &#215; (&#8730;2/2) = &#8730;2 v, so v = u/&#8730;2. The kinetic energy afterwards is 2 &#215; one half m (u/&#8730;2)&#178; = m u&#178;/2, which is the initial kinetic energy. The momentum along the original direction is 2m &#215; (u/&#8730;2) &#215; (1/&#8730;2) = mu, which is the initial momentum. Both laws are satisfied, so the answer is consistent.</p>
<p>This is the general result for equal masses with one at rest: the two outgoing paths are perpendicular. The symmetry imposed in this question then puts each of them at 45&#176; to the original direction, which is the special case of that general rule.</p>
<p><b>Answer: A</b></p>''',
    "trap": "The trap is to remember that the outgoing paths are perpendicular and then to report 90 degrees as the angle. The perpendicularity is a statement about the SUM of the two angles, not about either one of them, and here the symmetry splits the 90 degrees evenly.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 8 — E, materials.  Young's modulus, with the small-strain assumption made
#     explicit: the area is taken as its original value.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 8, "id": "S06-08", "module": "E", "diff": 3,
    "topic": "Young's modulus: finding a load from a measured extension, using the small-strain assumption",
    "rel": [("E", "Young's modulus is the stress divided by the strain, and the strain is the extension over the ORIGINAL length"),
            ("E", "The stress is the force divided by the cross-sectional area, and for a small strain the area is unchanged to good approximation"),
            ("C", "The load is a weight, so the force in newtons is the mass times g"),
            ("A", "Rearranging a proportionality to isolate the unknown before substituting keeps the arithmetic short")],
    "key": ["youngsmodulus", "strain", "stress", "smallstrain"],
    "stem": '<p>A steel wire of original length 2.0 m and cross-sectional area 1.0 &#215; 10<sup>&#8722;6</sup> m&#178; hangs vertically. A mass hung from its lower end stretches it by 1.0 mm. The Young modulus of the steel is 2.0 &#215; 10<sup>11</sup> Pa.</p><p>What is the mass?</p>',
    "opts": ['20 kg', '5.0 kg', '10 kg', '100 kg', '2.0 kg'],
    "ans": 2,
    "distractors": ['multiplies by the extension without dividing by the length, leaving out the strain altogether',
                    'halves the load by treating the wire as two wires sharing it',
                    'correct',
                    'reads the 1.0 mm extension as 1.0 cm, which is ten times too large',
                    'divides by the length twice, once inside the strain and once again when clearing the fraction'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "write Young&#8217;s modulus as stress over strain, with each of those written out in full"),
            ("solve", "compute the strain as the extension over the original length"),
            ("solve", "rearrange for the stress and multiply by the area to get the force"),
            ("relate", "convert the force to a mass by dividing by g")
        
        ],
        "relations": ["E = stress/strain = (F/A)/(&#916;&#8467;/&#8467;)",
                      "the strain is 1.0 &#215; 10 to the minus 3 m divided by 2.0 m, which is 5.0 &#215; 10 to the minus 4",
                      "F = E A &#916;&#8467;/&#8467; = 2.0 &#215; 10 to the 11 &#215; 1.0 &#215; 10 to the minus 6 &#215; 5.0 &#215; 10 to the minus 4 = 100 N",
                      "m = F/g = 100/10 = 10 kg"],
        "insight": "Young's modulus relates a stress to a strain, and the strain uses the ORIGINAL length. The extension is a thousandth of the length here, which is what makes the wire's own weight negligible beside the load and the cross-sectional area effectively unchanged: a strain of 5.0 &#215; 10 to the minus 4 changes the area by about one part in a thousand.",
        "shape": "proportionality",
        "approx": True,
        "symbolic": False,
        "figure_essential": False,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "2.0e11 * 1.0e-6 * (1.0e-3/2.0) / 10", "want": "10"},
    "sol": '''<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you write the strain with the original length, and whether you notice how small it is. The smallness is not decoration: it is what makes the answer a single multiplication.</p>
<p><b>Step 1 — write the definition in full.</b> Young&#8217;s modulus is</p>
<div class="formula">E = stress / strain = (F/A) / (&#916;&#8467;/&#8467;)</div>
<p>The strain is the extension divided by the <i>original</i> length. Dividing by the stretched length would be wrong, though here the two differ by so little that it would not change the answer &#8212; which is worth knowing, because it means you should not spend time worrying about it.</p>
<p><b>Step 2 — the strain.</b> Convert the extension to metres first:</p>
<div class="formula">&#916;&#8467; = 1.0 mm = 1.0 &#215; 10&#8722;&#179; m
strain = 1.0 &#215; 10&#8722;&#179; / 2.0 = 5.0 &#215; 10&#8722;&#8308;</div>
<p>A strain of five parts in ten thousand. The wire has stretched by 0.05 % of its length. This is the small-strain regime that the whole theory of Young&#8217;s modulus assumes, and it is why the cross-sectional area may be taken as its original value and the wire&#8217;s own weight negligible beside the load.</p>
<p><b>Step 3 — the stress.</b> Rearranging the definition:</p>
<div class="formula">stress = E &#215; strain = 2.0 &#215; 10&#185;&#185; &#215; 5.0 &#215; 10&#8722;&#8308; = 1.0 &#215; 10&#8312; Pa</div>
<p>That is 100 MPa, which is a sensible working stress for steel &#8212; well below its breaking stress of several hundred MPa, so the wire is in no danger of yielding.</p>
<p><b>Step 4 — the force, then the mass.</b></p>
<div class="formula">F = stress &#215; A = 1.0 &#215; 10&#8312; &#215; 1.0 &#215; 10&#8315;&#8310; = 100 N
m = F/g = 100/10 = 10 kg</div>
<p><b>Sanity check.</b> A 10 kg mass is a bucket of water, and it stretches a one square millimetre steel wire by a millimetre over two metres. That feels right: steel is stiff, and a millimetre is a very small extension for a wire two metres long. If your answer had come out as a tonne, the strain would have had to be a hundred times larger, which the given extension rules out.</p>
<p><b>Answer: C</b></p>''',
    "trap": "The trap is the unit of the extension. The strain is a ratio, and a ratio of lengths is dimensionless, so a candidate who leaves the extension in millimetres and the length in metres gets a strain a thousand times too small and a mass of 0.010 kg. Converting to metres before dividing is the whole of the discipline.",
},

